  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="income-content">
      
      <h2>What is Annual Income?</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Annual income</strong> (or yearly salary) is the cumulative total amount of money an individual earns over a 12-month calendar year from employment, contracting, or professional services. It is the primary financial metric evaluated by mortgage lenders, banks, landlords, tax authorities, and hiring managers when assessing financial capacity and job offers.
      </p>
      <p>
        For hourly employees, contractors, and freelancers, annual earnings fluctuate based on the exact number of hours worked per week and the number of paid versus unpaid weeks taken throughout the year.
      </p>

      <h2>How to Calculate Annual Income (Step-by-Step)</h2>
      <p>
        Calculating your gross yearly earnings requires three straightforward inputs:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Hourly Wage:</strong> The amount earned for every single hour of work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Working Hours per Week:</strong> The standard number of hours scheduled each week (e.g., 40 hours for standard full-time employment).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Working Weeks per Year:</strong> The number of weeks you receive payment (52 weeks for a full year, minus any unpaid leave or scheduled off-seasons).
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <strong>Master Annual Income Formula:</strong>
      </p>
      <pre><code>Annual Income = Hourly Wage × Hours per Week × Working Weeks per Year</code></pre>

      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-blue-200 bg-blue-50/50 p-4 dark:border-blue-900/50 dark:bg-blue-950/30">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-200 text-sm mb-1">Practical Calculation Walkthrough</h4>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Suppose an engineering contractor earns <strong>₹500 per hour</strong>, working <strong>40 hours per week</strong> across <strong>52 weeks</strong>:
        </p>
        <ul class="text-xs text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 mt-2 space-y-1">
          <li><strong>Weekly Income:</strong> ₹500 × 40 hours = <strong>₹20,000 per week</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Gross Annual Income:</strong> ₹20,000 × 52 weeks = <strong>₹10,40,000 per year</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Average Monthly Equivalent:</strong> ₹10,40,000 ÷ 12 months = <strong>₹86,666.67 per month</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>How to Calculate Annual Salary from Hourly Wage</h2>
      <p>
        To quickly convert an hourly wage into an annual salary for a standard full-time position (40 hours per week, 52 weeks per year):
      </p>
      <pre><code>Standard Annual Hours = 40 hours × 52 weeks = 2,080 hours per year</code></pre>
      <p>
        Simply multiply your hourly pay rate by <strong>2,080</strong>:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>₹300/hour × 2,080 = <strong>₹6,24,000 / year</strong></li>
        <li>₹500/hour × 2,080 = <strong>₹10,40,000 / year</strong></li>
        <li>₹750/hour × 2,080 = <strong>₹15,60,000 / year</strong></li>
        <li>₹1,000/hour × 2,080 = <strong>₹20,80,000 / year</strong></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Gross Annual Income vs. Net Annual Income</h2>
      <p>
        Understanding the vital distinction between gross and net income ensures realistic household budgeting:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Gross Annual Income:</strong> The raw top-line compensation negotiated with your employer or client before any deductions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Net Annual Income (Take-Home Pay):</strong> The actual cash deposited into your bank account after mandatory deductions, including income tax withholding, social security, pension/EPF/PPF contributions, and health insurance.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <strong>Simplified Net Income Formula:</strong>
      </p>
      <pre><code>Net Annual Income = Gross Annual Income × (1 - Tax Rate / 100)</code></pre>
      <p>
        <em>Note:</em> Actual take-home pay depends on progressive statutory income tax slabs, exemptions, standard deductions, and individual declarations. This calculator provides a clean, generalized tax rate estimation.
      </p>

      <h2>How Unpaid Leave and Holidays Affect Annual Income</h2>
      <p>
        If your job or contract does not offer paid time off (PTO), taking vacations or sick leave directly diminishes your annual pay. 
        Instead of multiplying by 52 weeks, subtract your unpaid weeks first:
      </p>
      <pre><code>Working Weeks = 52 - Unpaid Leave Weeks</code></pre>
      <p>
        <strong>Example:</strong> Taking 2 weeks of unpaid leave reduces your annual working weeks to 50:
      </p>
      <pre><code>Annual Income = ₹500 × 40 hrs × 50 wks = ₹10,00,000</code></pre>
      <p>
        Those 2 unpaid weeks cost <code>₹500 × 40 × 2 = ₹40,000</code> in lost annual gross income.
      </p>

      <h2>How Much Do I Need to Earn Per Hour to Make a Specific Annual Salary?</h2>
      <p>
        If you have a target annual revenue goal in mind, you can reverse-calculate the required hourly wage:
      </p>
      <pre><code>Required Hourly Wage = Desired Annual Income / (Hours per Week × Working Weeks per Year)</code></pre>
      <p>
        <strong>Example:</strong> To achieve a gross annual income of <strong>₹12,00,000</strong> while working 48 weeks at 50 hours per week:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Calculate total billable hours: <code>50 hrs × 48 wks = 2,400 hours</code>.</li>
        <li>Divide target salary by total hours: <code>₹12,00,000 ÷ 2,400 = ₹500.00/hour</code>.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Salary Period Conversion Breakdown</h2>
      <p>
        Salaries can be expressed across multiple standard payment schedules:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Hourly:</strong> Base unit of pay per 60 minutes of labor.</li>
        <li><strong>Daily (8 Hours):</strong> <code>Hourly Wage × 8</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Weekly (40 Hours):</strong> <code>Hourly Wage × 40</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Bi-Weekly (Every 2 Weeks):</strong> <code>Weekly Income × 2</code> (26 paychecks per year).</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly Equivalent:</strong> <code>Annual Income ÷ 12</code> (note: months have an average of 4.33 weeks, so monthly pay is not simply weekly pay × 4).</li>
        <li><strong>Annual:</strong> Total yearly earnings across all working weeks.</li>
      </ul>

